mpts']\n    max_attempts = case['max_attempts']\n    last = attempts[-1]\n    if last['success']:\n        return {\"decision\": \"stop\", \"memory_append\": None, \"next_attempt\": -1}\n    if len(attempts) >= max_attempts:\n        return {\"decision\": \"stop\", \"memory_append\": None, \"next_attempt\": -1}\n    return {\n        \"decision\": \"continue\",\n        \"memory_append\": {\"reflection\": last['reflection'], \"signature\": last['failure_signature']},\n        \"next_attempt\": len(attempts)\n    }\n\nWe should also handle empty attempts? Not in fixtures, but to be safe we could assume at least one.\n\nEnsure we don't use external libs.\n\nNow output as JSON string with escaping.\n\nWe need to produce final answer as a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value as string of Python code. The entire response must be exactly that JSON object.\n\nLet's construct the JSON string manually.\n\nWe'll write:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    attempts = case['attempts']\\n    max_attempts = case['max_attempts']\\n    last = attempts[-1]\\n    if last['success']:\\n        return {\\\"decision\\\": \\\"stop\\\", \\\"memory_append\\\": None, \\\"next_attempt\\\": -1}\\n    if len(attempts) >= max_attempts:\\n        return {\\\"decision\\\": \\\"stop\\\", \\\"memory_append\\\": None, \\\"next_attempt\\\": -1}\\n    return {\\n        \\\"decision\\\": \\\"continue\\\",\\n        \\\"memory_append\\\": {\\\"reflection\\\": last['reflection'], \\\"signature\\\": last['failure_signature']},\\n        \\\"next_attempt\\\": len(attempts)\\n    }\"}\n\nBut need to escape quotes inside the string. In JSON, double quotes inside string must be escaped as \\\". Also newlines as \\n. Backslashes as \\\\. So we need to replace each double quote with \\\". Also the code contains single quotes which are fine. But we have double quotes inside dict literals. So our string should use escaped double quotes inside.\n\nWrite the code with double quotes for dict keys? We can use single quotes in Python to avoid escaping? But then the string will contain single quotes, which are fine in JSON. However, we need to output a JSON string that contains Python source code. The Python source can use single quotes for strings, which is easier. Then we don't need to escape double quotes inside. The JSON string will be enclosed in double quotes, and we need to escape any double quotes inside if we use them. But if we use single quotes in Python code, there are no double quotes inside the Python source. However, we do have the return dicts that use double quotes as keys? Actually, we used double quotes for keys like \"decision\". That's fine if we use single quotes in Python? No, the dict keys are strings; they can be single-quoted. We can write {'decision': 'stop'} etc. So we can avoid double quotes entirely in the Python source. That makes JSON escaping simpler: no need to escape double quotes. Only need to escape backslashes and control characters. But we have newlines: we need to represent them as \\n in JSON. That's fine. So we'll write Python code using single quotes for all strings. Then the JSON string will contain single quotes, which are not special inside JSON. We also have None (Python None) - that's fine.\n\nSo revised Python code using single quotes:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    attempts = case['attempts']\n    max_attempts = case['max_attempts']\n    last = attempts[-1]\n    if last['success']:\n        return {'decision': 'stop', 'memory_append': None, 'next_attempt': -1}\n    if len(attempts) >= max_attempts:\n        return {'decision': 'stop', 'memory_append': None, 'next_attempt': -1}\n    return {\n        'decision': 'continue',\n        'memory_append': {'reflection': last['reflection'], 'signature': last['failure_signature']},\n        'next_attempt': len(attempts)\n    }\n\nThis has no double quotes. Good.\n\nNow create JSON string: we need to embed this code as a string. In JSON, we need to escape backslashes and newlines. The code has single quotes, not special. Also there is a colon inside the code which is fine. The newlines we represent as \\n.\n\nLet's write the Python source as a single line with \\n escapes. Or we can keep multiline string in JSON? JSON strings can have literal newlines? Actually, JSON spec says a string can contain any Unicode character except \" or \\ or control characters. Newline is a control character, so it must be escaped as \\n. So we need to replace newlines with \\n.\n\nSo we can write the code as one line with \\n. But for readability, we can write it as usual in our editor and then convert. But the final output must be a valid JSON string. So we'll produce a string with escaped newlines.\n\nI'll write the code string with explicit \\n sequences.\n\nThus:\n\n\"def solve(case):\\n    attempts = case['attempts']\\n    max_attempts = case['max_attempts']\\n    last = attempts[-1]\\n    if last['success']:\\n        return {'decision': 'stop', 'memory_append': None, 'next_attempt': -1}\\n    if len(attempts) >= max_attempts:\\n        return {'decision': 'stop', 'memory_append': None, 'next_attempt': -1}\\n    return {\\n        'decision': 'continue',\\n        'memory_append': {'reflection': last['reflection'], 'signature': last['failure_signature']},\\n        'next_attempt': len(attempts)\\n    }\"\n\nNote: there are newlines after return { and after the dict items. We need to escape those as \\n. Also the indentation spaces are fine.\n\nNow put this into a JSON object with key \"implementation\".\n\nWe must ensure no trailing spaces or extra text.\n\nSo final output:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    attempts = case['attempts']\\n    max_attempts = case['max_attempts']\\n    last = attempts[-1]\\n    if last['success']:\\n        return {'decision': 'stop', 'memory_append': None, 'next_attempt': -1}\\n    if len(attempts) >= max_attempts:\\n        return {'decision': 'stop', 'memory_append': None, 'next_attempt': -1}\\n    return {\\n        'decision': 'continue',\\n        'memory_append': {'reflection': last['reflection'], 'signature': last['failure_signature']},\\n        'next_attempt': len(attempts)\\n    }\"}\n\nCheck: there are double quotes around the string, and inside the string we have single quotes, no double quotes, so no need for \\\". Also backslashes? The string contains newlines escaped as \\n, that's fine. No other escaping needed.\n\nWe should also consider that the case payload may have other fields like \"_case_nonce\" which we ignore. That's fine.\n\nThus final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1155,"completion_tokens":3041,"total_tokens":4196,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1152},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2871},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1152,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":3},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}